IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT domain
IMPORT `g2-nouns`
IMPORT `g2-art6-purposes`
IMPORT `g2-art7-public-benefit`

-- g2-art5-charity-test.l4: Article 5 of the Charities (Jersey) Law 2014, The charity test: the test, the bar on control by a Minister or a member of the States Assembly, the Minister's power
-- to disapply it, and the guidance duties.  Group G2.  Imports only prelude, daydate, domain and the G2 modules `g2-nouns`, `g2-art6-purposes` and `g2-art7-public-benefit`.  No section GIVEN.
--
-- READING THE TEST.  Article 5(1) says an entity meets the test if (a) all of its purposes are charitable purposes or purposes purely ancillary or incidental to its charitable purposes, and
-- (b) in giving effect to those purposes it provides (an applicant: provides or intends to provide) public benefit to a reasonable degree.  Article 5(2) then says that an entity that
-- OTHERWISE meets the test does not meet it if its constitution expressly permits control by, or a governor to be, a Minister, a member of the States Assembly or an equivalent, acting in
-- that capacity, unless an Order under 5(3) disapplies 5(2).
--   * (a) is decided from the purposes (g2-art6-purposes decides each one); (b) is the determiner's finding, constrained by Article 7 (g2-art7-public-benefit); 5(2) from the constitution.
--   * A barred entity does not meet the test whether or not (a) and (b) hold, so `Art 5 the entity meets the charity test` asks the bar first: it answers FALSE for a barred entity even where
--     (b) cannot be answered.  `Art 5 the limb that decides the charity test` names the first limb, in the order the text states them, that the entity does not meet; for an entity that is
--     barred and whose (b) cannot be answered it refuses, because it cannot tell whether (b) or 5(2) is the first limb that fails.
--   * A body that is not an entity (`kinds of entity` empty: it falls within none of the sub-paragraphs of Article 2(1)) does not meet the test, whatever its purposes.  Article 5(1) states the test for "an
--     entity".  `Art 5(1) the body is an entity within Article 2(1)` is asked FIRST by the exports that answer "does the entity meet the test" (IP8, IP10, the limb function), which answer FALSE (the limb
--     function names `Article 5(1) does not apply ...`); it is not a refusal.  Added after independent testing.
--   * `Purpose` in domain.l4 does not carry the facts that 6(2) reads, so the charity test takes the entity's purposes TOGETHER WITH those facts, and checks that they are the entity's purposes.

§ `Part 3 — Charity Test`

§§ `Article 5 — The charity test`

-- A refusal, named for what it says: the list of purposes with their facts is not the entity's list of purposes.
@ref Article 5(1)(a) (src:146-148)
GIVEN a IS A TYPE
GIVETH AN a
`the purposes with their Article 6 facts are not the purposes of the entity` MEANS
    REFUSE "the purposes with their Article 6 facts are not the purposes of the entity"

@ref Article 5(1)(a) (src:146-148)
GIVEN `the entity` IS AN Entity
      pfs IS A LIST OF `Purpose with the facts Article 6 reads`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the purposes with their Article 6 facts are the purposes of the entity` `the entity` pfs MEANS
    (map (GIVEN pf YIELD pf's purpose) pfs) EQUALS `the entity`'s purposes

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

§§§ `Article 5(1) — The charity test`

-- src:145 | (1) An entity meets the charity test if –
-- src:146 | (a) all of its purposes are –
-- src:147 | (i) charitable purposes, or
-- src:148 | (ii) purposes that are purely ancillary or incidental to any of its charitable purposes; and
-- src:149 | (b) in giving effect to those purposes, it provides (or, in the case of an applicant, provides or intends to provide) public benefit in Jersey or elsewhere to a reasonable degree.

-- src:201 | (5) The purpose of advancing a political party or promoting a candidate for election to any office, whether in Jersey or elsewhere, is neither a charitable purpose nor a purpose ancillary or incidental to a charitable purpose, irrespective of whether it would otherwise fall within paragraph (1) or Article 5(1)(a)(ii).
-- 5(1)(a)(i) and (ii) for one purpose.  A political purpose is neither (Article 6(5)): the exclusion reaches the ancillary limb by its own words.
@ref Article 5(1)(a)(i) (src:147)
GIVEN interp IS AN `Interpretation of Articles 3 to 7`
      pf IS A `Purpose with the facts Article 6 reads`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Art 5(1)(a)(i) the purpose is a charitable purpose` interp pf MEANS
    `Art 6(1) the purpose with its facts is a charitable purpose` interp pf

@ref Article 5(1)(a)(ii), Article 6(5) (src:148) (src:201)
GIVEN pf IS A `Purpose with the facts Article 6 reads`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Art 5(1)(a)(ii) the purpose is purely ancillary or incidental to a charitable purpose` pf MEANS
        pf's purpose's `is purely ancillary or incidental to a charitable purpose of the entity`
    AND NOT `Art 6(5) the purpose is neither charitable nor ancillary` (pf's purpose)

-- FORK F2.1.  Whether an ancillary purpose can count at all depends on there being a charitable purpose for it to be ancillary to ("any of its charitable purposes"): on the first and
-- second readings it counts only beside one; on the third it counts alone.
@ref Article 5(1)(a)(ii) (src:148)
GIVEN interp IS AN `Interpretation of Articles 3 to 7`
      pfs IS A LIST OF `Purpose with the facts Article 6 reads`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Art 5(1)(a)(ii) an ancillary purpose can count` interp pfs MEANS
    CONSIDER interp's `how the purposes of an entity are tested`
    WHEN `at least one charitable purpose is required, and an ancillary purpose counts only beside one`
        THEN any (GIVEN pf YIELD `Art 5(1)(a)(i) the purpose is a charitable purpose` interp pf) pfs
    WHEN `an entity with no purposes satisfies the test vacuously`
        THEN any (GIVEN pf YIELD `Art 5(1)(a)(i) the purpose is a charitable purpose` interp pf) pfs
    WHEN `purely ancillary purposes satisfy the test without any charitable purpose`
        THEN TRUE

@ref Article 5(1)(a) (src:146-148)
GIVEN interp IS AN `Interpretation of Articles 3 to 7`
      pfs IS A LIST OF `Purpose with the facts Article 6 reads`
      pf IS A `Purpose with the facts Article 6 reads`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Art 5(1)(a) the purpose counts` interp pfs pf MEANS
        "(i)" ... `Art 5(1)(a)(i) the purpose is a charitable purpose` interp pf
    OR  "(ii)" ... (    `Art 5(1)(a)(ii) the purpose is purely ancillary or incidental to a charitable purpose` pf
                    AND `Art 5(1)(a)(ii) an ancillary purpose can count` interp pfs)

-- The purposes that are neither charitable nor purely ancillary, on any reading: the ones that make (a) fail whatever F2.1 says.  (A purely ancillary purpose with nothing charitable beside
-- it is not in this list; on the first two readings of F2.1 it does not count, and then it is the absence of a charitable purpose that stops (a) being met.)
@ref Article 5(1)(a) (src:146-148)
GIVEN interp IS AN `Interpretation of Articles 3 to 7`
      pfs IS A LIST OF `Purpose with the facts Article 6 reads`
GIVETH A LIST OF Purpose
`Art 5(1)(a) the purposes of the entity that are neither charitable nor purely ancillary` interp pfs MEANS
    map (GIVEN pf YIELD pf's purpose)
        (filter (GIVEN pf YIELD NOT (    `Art 5(1)(a)(i) the purpose is a charitable purpose` interp pf
                                     OR `Art 5(1)(a)(ii) the purpose is purely ancillary or incidental to a charitable purpose` pf)) pfs)

-- 5(1)(a): all of the entity's purposes are charitable purposes or purely ancillary or incidental to them.  FORK F2.1 asks what "all" does with no purposes, and with ancillary purposes alone.
@ref Article 5(1)(a) (src:146-148)
@export Whether all of an entity's purposes are charitable purposes or purposes purely ancillary or incidental to its charitable purposes (Article 5(1)(a)), on the reading of "all of its purposes" chosen by the interpretation
GIVEN interp IS AN `Interpretation of Articles 3 to 7`
        @desc The readings of the ambiguous provisions of Articles 3 to 7; the field read here is how the purposes of an entity are tested
      `the entity` IS AN Entity
        @desc The entity as Article 2 leaves it; its purposes are the ones tested
      `the purposes with their Article 6 facts` IS A LIST OF `Purpose with the facts Article 6 reads`
        @desc The entity's purposes, in the same order, each with the facts that Article 6(2) reads about it
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Art 5(1)(a) all of the entity's purposes are charitable or purely ancillary` interp `the entity` `the purposes with their Article 6 facts` MEANS
    IF NOT `the purposes with their Article 6 facts are the purposes of the entity` `the entity` `the purposes with their Article 6 facts`
    THEN `the purposes with their Article 6 facts are not the purposes of the entity`
    ELSE CONSIDER interp's `how the purposes of an entity are tested`
         WHEN `at least one charitable purpose is required, and an ancillary purpose counts only beside one`
             THEN `reading A of Article 5(1)(a): a charitable purpose is required` interp `the purposes with their Article 6 facts`
         WHEN `an entity with no purposes satisfies the test vacuously`
             THEN `reading B of Article 5(1)(a): vacuous truth` interp `the purposes with their Article 6 facts`
         WHEN `purely ancillary purposes satisfy the test without any charitable purpose`
             THEN `reading C of Article 5(1)(a): ancillary purposes alone` interp `the purposes with their Article 6 facts`

@ref Article 5(1)(a) (src:146-148)
GIVEN interp IS AN `Interpretation of Articles 3 to 7`
      pfs IS A LIST OF `Purpose with the facts Article 6 reads`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`reading A of Article 5(1)(a): a charitable purpose is required` interp pfs MEANS
        any (GIVEN pf YIELD `Art 5(1)(a)(i) the purpose is a charitable purpose` interp pf) pfs
    AND all (GIVEN pf YIELD `Art 5(1)(a) the purpose counts` interp pfs pf) pfs

@ref Article 5(1)(a) (src:146-148)
GIVEN interp IS AN `Interpretation of Articles 3 to 7`
      pfs IS A LIST OF `Purpose with the facts Article 6 reads`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`reading B of Article 5(1)(a): vacuous truth` interp pfs MEANS
    all (GIVEN pf YIELD `Art 5(1)(a) the purpose counts` interp pfs pf) pfs

@ref Article 5(1)(a) (src:146-148)
GIVEN interp IS AN `Interpretation of Articles 3 to 7`
      pfs IS A LIST OF `Purpose with the facts Article 6 reads`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`reading C of Article 5(1)(a): ancillary purposes alone` interp pfs MEANS
    all (GIVEN pf YIELD `Art 5(1)(a) the purpose counts` interp pfs pf) pfs

-- 5(1)(b): in giving effect to those purposes the entity provides (an applicant: provides or intends to provide) public benefit in Jersey or elsewhere to a reasonable degree.
-- The finding is the determiner's; Article 7 constrains it and decides the cases it takes out of the determiner's hands.
@ref Article 5(1)(b) (src:149)
@export Whether the entity provides public benefit in Jersey or elsewhere to a reasonable degree (or, for an applicant, provides or intends to provide it), as Article 5(1)(b) asks, on a determination of the question
GIVEN interp IS AN `Interpretation of Articles 3 to 7`
        @desc The readings of the ambiguous provisions of Articles 3 to 7; the field read here is what follows from a determination made in breach of the duties on its maker
      `the entity` IS AN Entity
        @desc The entity as Article 2 leaves it; its standing on the register says whether it is an applicant
      `the public benefit determination` IS A `Determination of public benefit`
        @desc The determination: who made it, what it found, and what its maker did and did not do
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Art 5(1)(b) the entity provides public benefit to a reasonable degree` interp `the entity` `the public benefit determination` MEANS
    `Art 7 the entity provides public benefit` interp `the entity` `the public benefit determination`

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

§§§ `Article 5(2) — The bar on control by a Minister or a member of the States Assembly`

-- src:150 | (2) An entity that otherwise meets the charity test, nevertheless does not meet that test, despite paragraph (1), if its constitution expressly permits its activities to be directed or otherwise controlled by, or any of its governors to be –
-- src:151 | (a) a Minister;
-- src:152 | (b) a member of the States Assembly; or
-- src:153 | (c) any equivalent of such a person in another jurisdiction,
-- src:154 | acting in that capacity.

-- src:26 | “Minister” means the Chief Minister;
-- Whether the person a permission names is one that Article 5(2) catches.  FORK F2.3: "a Minister" in (a) is any Minister of the Government of Jersey (first reading, taken), or only
-- "the Minister" as Article 1 defines the term, which names an office that the text in force chooses (second reading).  For (b) and (c) there is nothing to read.
@ref Article 5(2)(a) to (c), Article 1 "Minister" (src:151-153) (src:26)
GIVEN interp IS AN `Interpretation of Articles 3 to 7`
      `the office of the Minister` IS AN `Office of the Minister`
      person IS A `Person named in Article 5(2)`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Art 5(2) the person is one that Article 5(2) names` interp `the office of the Minister` person MEANS
    CONSIDER person
    WHEN `a Minister` office
        THEN CONSIDER interp's `what a Minister means in Article 5(2)(a)`
             WHEN `any Minister of the Government of Jersey` THEN TRUE
             WHEN `only the Minister, as Article 1 defines the term in the text in force` THEN office EQUALS JUST `the office of the Minister`
    WHEN `a member of the States Assembly` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `any equivalent of a Minister or of a member of the States Assembly in another jurisdiction` THEN TRUE

-- Whether "acting in that capacity" is satisfied by the permission.  FORK F2.2: the person must be permitted to act as a Minister, a member or an equivalent (first reading, taken), or any
-- such person is caught however the person is to act (second reading).
@ref Article 5(2) (src:154)
GIVEN interp IS AN `Interpretation of Articles 3 to 7`
      permission IS A `Permission that a constitution expressly makes`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Art 5(2) the permission is for the person to act in that capacity` interp permission MEANS
    CONSIDER interp's `how acting in that capacity qualifies the persons named in Article 5(2)`
    WHEN `the person must be permitted to act as a Minister, a member of the States Assembly or an equivalent` THEN permission's `acting in that capacity`
    WHEN `any such person is caught however the person is to act` THEN TRUE

-- Whether one permission is within Article 5(2): the constitution expressly permits the activities of the entity to be directed or otherwise controlled by, or any of its governors to be,
-- a person that 5(2) names, acting in that capacity.  Both permissions the text names are caught, so the kind of permission is not read.
@ref Article 5(2) (src:150-154)
GIVEN interp IS AN `Interpretation of Articles 3 to 7`
      `the office of the Minister` IS AN `Office of the Minister`
      permission IS A `Permission that a constitution expressly makes`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Art 5(2) the permission is one that Article 5(2) catches` interp `the office of the Minister` permission MEANS
        `Art 5(2) the person is one that Article 5(2) names` interp `the office of the Minister` (permission's `the person permitted`)
    AND `Art 5(2) the permission is for the person to act in that capacity` interp permission

@ref Article 5(2) (src:150-154)
@export Whether the constitution of an entity expressly permits what Article 5(2) forbids: the activities of the entity to be directed or otherwise controlled by, or any of its governors to be, a Minister, a member of the States Assembly or an equivalent in another jurisdiction, acting in that capacity; the readings of "a Minister" and of "acting in that capacity" are chosen by the interpretation
GIVEN interp IS AN `Interpretation of Articles 3 to 7`
        @desc The readings of the ambiguous provisions of Articles 3 to 7; the fields read here are what "a Minister" means and how "acting in that capacity" qualifies the persons named
      `the office of the Minister` IS AN `Office of the Minister`
        @desc Which office Article 1 calls "the Minister" in the text of the Law in force on the day in question; read only on the second reading of fork F2.3
      `the constitution facts` IS A `Facts about the constitution under Article 5(2)`
        @desc The permissions of the kinds Article 5(2) names that the text of the entity's constitution expressly makes; empty if it makes none
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Art 5(2) the constitution defeats the charity test` interp `the office of the Minister` `the constitution facts` MEANS
    any (GIVEN permission YIELD `Art 5(2) the permission is one that Article 5(2) catches` interp `the office of the Minister` permission) (`the constitution facts`'s `permissions that the constitution expressly makes`)

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

§§§ `Article 5(3) — The Minister's power to disapply paragraph (2)`

-- src:155 | (3) The Minister may by Order disapply paragraph (2) in relation to any entity or description of entity specified in the Order.
@ref Article 5(3) (src:155)
@export The Minister's permission under Article 5(3) to make an Order disapplying Article 5(2) in relation to an entity or a description of entity; the Order's content is not modelled here, and an unused permission is not a breach
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Legal actor` `Act under Articles 3 to 7`
`Art 5(3) the Minister may disapply Article 5(2)` MEANS
    PARTY `the Minister`
    MAY `make an Order disapplying the bar on control by a Minister, a member of the States Assembly or an equivalent` `the Minister`
    HENCE FULFILLED

-- Whether Article 5(2) bars the entity: its constitution is within 5(2) and no Order under 5(3) disapplies 5(2) for it.  Which entities an Order specifies is the Order's content, an input.
@ref Article 5(2), Article 5(3) (src:150-155)
@export Whether Article 5(2) bars the entity from meeting the charity test: its constitution expressly permits what Article 5(2) forbids, and no Order under Article 5(3) disapplies Article 5(2) for the entity
GIVEN interp IS AN `Interpretation of Articles 3 to 7`
        @desc The readings of the ambiguous provisions of Articles 3 to 7; the fields read here are what "a Minister" means and how "acting in that capacity" qualifies the persons named
      `the office of the Minister` IS AN `Office of the Minister`
        @desc Which office Article 1 calls "the Minister" in the text of the Law in force on the day in question; read only on the second reading of fork F2.3
      `the constitution facts` IS A `Facts about the constitution under Article 5(2)`
        @desc The permissions of the kinds Article 5(2) names that the text of the entity's constitution expressly makes; empty if it makes none
      `an Order under Article 5(3) disapplies Article 5(2) for the entity` IS A BOOLEAN
        @desc TRUE if an Order made by the Minister under Article 5(3) specifies the entity, or a description of entity that it falls within, as one to which Article 5(2) does not apply
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Art 5(2) the bar applies to the entity` interp `the office of the Minister` `the constitution facts` `an Order under Article 5(3) disapplies Article 5(2) for the entity` MEANS
        `Art 5(2) the constitution defeats the charity test` interp `the office of the Minister` `the constitution facts`
    AND NOT `an Order under Article 5(3) disapplies Article 5(2) for the entity`

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

§§§ `Article 5 — Whether the entity meets the charity test, and which limb decides it`

-- Article 5(1) states the test for "an entity".  A body that falls within none of the sub-paragraphs of Article 2(1) is not an entity (domain.l4: `kinds of entity` is empty if it is not one),
-- so it cannot meet the test, and the exports that answer "does the entity meet the charity test" answer FALSE for it, whatever its purposes; they do not refuse, because the Law states the
-- test only for an entity and so a non-entity simply does not meet it.  Added after independent testing; the guard comes before the coverage check because it does not read the purposes.
@ref Article 5(1) (src:145)
GIVEN `the entity` IS AN Entity
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Art 5(1) the body is an entity within Article 2(1)` `the entity` MEANS
    NOT null (`the entity`'s `kinds of entity`)

-- IP8.  Asks the bar first (see the header): a barred entity does not meet the test whether or not (a) and (b) hold.  Then (a), then (b), left to right, so that an entity that fails (a) is
-- answered FALSE without (b) being consulted.
@ref Article 5(1), Article 5(2), Article 5(3) (src:145-155)
@export Whether an entity meets the charity test of Article 5: all of its purposes are charitable purposes or purely ancillary or incidental to them, it provides public benefit to a reasonable degree, and Article 5(2) does not bar it; a body that is not an entity (no kind of entity under Article 2(1)) does not meet it, and the answer for such a body is FALSE, not a refusal; the readings of the ambiguous provisions are chosen by the interpretation
GIVEN interp IS AN `Interpretation of Articles 3 to 7`
        @desc The readings of the ambiguous provisions of Articles 3 to 7
      `the entity` IS AN Entity
        @desc The entity as Article 2 leaves it: its standing on the register and its purposes
      `the purposes with their Article 6 facts` IS A LIST OF `Purpose with the facts Article 6 reads`
        @desc The entity's purposes, in the same order, each with the facts that Article 6(2) reads about it
      `the constitution facts` IS A `Facts about the constitution under Article 5(2)`
        @desc The permissions of the kinds Article 5(2) names that the text of the entity's constitution expressly makes; empty if it makes none
      `the office of the Minister` IS AN `Office of the Minister`
        @desc Which office Article 1 calls "the Minister" in the text of the Law in force on the day in question; read only on the second reading of fork F2.3
      `the public benefit determination` IS A `Determination of public benefit`
        @desc The determination of whether the entity provides, or intends to provide, public benefit: who made it, what it found, and what its maker did and did not do
      `an Order under Article 5(3) disapplies Article 5(2) for the entity` IS A BOOLEAN
        @desc TRUE if an Order made by the Minister under Article 5(3) specifies the entity, or a description of entity that it falls within, as one to which Article 5(2) does not apply
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Art 5 the entity meets the charity test` interp `the entity` `the purposes with their Article 6 facts` `the constitution facts` `the office of the Minister` `the public benefit determination` `an Order under Article 5(3) disapplies Article 5(2) for the entity` MEANS
    IF NOT `Art 5(1) the body is an entity within Article 2(1)` `the entity`
    THEN FALSE
    ELSE
        IF NOT `the purposes with their Article 6 facts are the purposes of the entity` `the entity` `the purposes with their Article 6 facts`
        THEN `the purposes with their Article 6 facts are not the purposes of the entity`
        ELSE
                    NOT `Art 5(2) the bar applies to the entity` interp `the office of the Minister` `the constitution facts` `an Order under Article 5(3) disapplies Article 5(2) for the entity`
                AND "(1)(a)" ... `Art 5(1)(a) all of the entity's purposes are charitable or purely ancillary` interp `the entity` `the purposes with their Article 6 facts`
                AND "(1)(b)" ... `Art 5(1)(b) the entity provides public benefit to a reasonable degree` interp `the entity` `the public benefit determination`

-- IP10 (consumed by group G3, Article 8(3)(i)).  The entity meets the charity test only by virtue of an Order: its constitution is within 5(2), an Order under 5(3) disapplies 5(2) for it,
-- and (a) and (b) hold, so that without the Order it would have been barred.
@ref Article 5(2), Article 5(3) (src:150-155)
@export Whether an entity meets the charity test only by virtue of an Order under Article 5(3): its constitution is within Article 5(2), an Order disapplies Article 5(2) for it, and it otherwise meets the test; FALSE for a body that is not an entity
GIVEN interp IS AN `Interpretation of Articles 3 to 7`
        @desc The readings of the ambiguous provisions of Articles 3 to 7
      `the entity` IS AN Entity
        @desc The entity as Article 2 leaves it: its standing on the register and its purposes
      `the purposes with their Article 6 facts` IS A LIST OF `Purpose with the facts Article 6 reads`
        @desc The entity's purposes, in the same order, each with the facts that Article 6(2) reads about it
      `the constitution facts` IS A `Facts about the constitution under Article 5(2)`
        @desc The permissions of the kinds Article 5(2) names that the text of the entity's constitution expressly makes; empty if it makes none
      `the office of the Minister` IS AN `Office of the Minister`
        @desc Which office Article 1 calls "the Minister" in the text of the Law in force on the day in question; read only on the second reading of fork F2.3
      `the public benefit determination` IS A `Determination of public benefit`
        @desc The determination of whether the entity provides, or intends to provide, public benefit
      `an Order under Article 5(3) disapplies Article 5(2) for the entity` IS A BOOLEAN
        @desc TRUE if an Order made by the Minister under Article 5(3) specifies the entity, or a description of entity that it falls within, as one to which Article 5(2) does not apply
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Art 5(3) the entity meets the charity test only by virtue of an Order` interp `the entity` `the purposes with their Article 6 facts` `the constitution facts` `the office of the Minister` `the public benefit determination` `an Order under Article 5(3) disapplies Article 5(2) for the entity` MEANS
    IF NOT `Art 5(1) the body is an entity within Article 2(1)` `the entity`
    THEN FALSE
    ELSE
        IF NOT `the purposes with their Article 6 facts are the purposes of the entity` `the entity` `the purposes with their Article 6 facts`
        THEN `the purposes with their Article 6 facts are not the purposes of the entity`
        ELSE
                    `an Order under Article 5(3) disapplies Article 5(2) for the entity`
                AND `Art 5(2) the constitution defeats the charity test` interp `the office of the Minister` `the constitution facts`
                AND `Art 5(1)(a) all of the entity's purposes are charitable or purely ancillary` interp `the entity` `the purposes with their Article 6 facts`
                AND `Art 5(1)(b) the entity provides public benefit to a reasonable degree` interp `the entity` `the public benefit determination`

-- Which limb decides the charity test: the first, in the order the text states them (5(1)(a), 5(1)(b), then 5(2) for an entity that otherwise meets the test), that the entity does not meet.
@ref Article 5(1), Article 5(2), Article 5(3) (src:145-155)
@export Which limb of the charity test of Article 5 decides it, taken in the order the text states them: Article 5(1)(a), Article 5(1)(b), then Article 5(2) for an entity that otherwise meets the test; or that the entity meets the test; or that the body is not an entity within Article 2(1), so that Article 5(1) does not apply to it and it does not meet the test
GIVEN interp IS AN `Interpretation of Articles 3 to 7`
        @desc The readings of the ambiguous provisions of Articles 3 to 7
      `the entity` IS AN Entity
        @desc The entity as Article 2 leaves it: its standing on the register and its purposes
      `the purposes with their Article 6 facts` IS A LIST OF `Purpose with the facts Article 6 reads`
        @desc The entity's purposes, in the same order, each with the facts that Article 6(2) reads about it
      `the constitution facts` IS A `Facts about the constitution under Article 5(2)`
        @desc The permissions of the kinds Article 5(2) names that the text of the entity's constitution expressly makes; empty if it makes none
      `the office of the Minister` IS AN `Office of the Minister`
        @desc Which office Article 1 calls "the Minister" in the text of the Law in force on the day in question; read only on the second reading of fork F2.3
      `the public benefit determination` IS A `Determination of public benefit`
        @desc The determination of whether the entity provides, or intends to provide, public benefit
      `an Order under Article 5(3) disapplies Article 5(2) for the entity` IS A BOOLEAN
        @desc TRUE if an Order made by the Minister under Article 5(3) specifies the entity, or a description of entity that it falls within, as one to which Article 5(2) does not apply
GIVETH A `Limb of the charity test that decides it`
`Art 5 the limb that decides the charity test` interp `the entity` `the purposes with their Article 6 facts` `the constitution facts` `the office of the Minister` `the public benefit determination` `an Order under Article 5(3) disapplies Article 5(2) for the entity` MEANS
